# The build stage does `COPY . .` on top of the node_modules it installed in
# the deps stage, so anything not excluded here is not merely wasted context --
# it lands in the image and can overwrite what the build needs.
#
# Three entries are load-bearing rather than tidy:
#
#   node_modules  the deps stage runs `npm ci` against the lockfile and the
#                 build stage copies that result in. A host tree copied over it
#                 replaces a Linux install with whatever the developer's OS
#                 produced, which for any package with a native binding is the
#                 wrong architecture.
#   .next         a host build's output. `npm run build` would start from it,
#                 so a stale local build could reach the image.
#   NUL           not a typo. npm on Windows materialises its cache under this
#                 reserved name when a command redirects to NUL from a POSIX
#                 shell, and the Docker daemon cannot read the resulting path
#                 at all: the build fails with "error from sender: open
#                 frontend\NUL: Incorrect function" before any layer runs. It
#                 is gitignored, so it is invisible in `git status` and looks
#                 like nothing is wrong.
node_modules
.next
out
build
NUL

.git
.gitignore
coverage
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

.env
.env.*

npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.pem
.vercel
